Roasted Carrot Pasta
  --Richard Makin

450 g carrots (around 4 carrots), peeled and sliced into coins
1 medium onion, roughly chopped
2 tbsp olive oil
1 tbsp miso paste (white or red)
1/2 tsp fine sea salt
1/4 tsp ground black pepper
1 bulb garlic
160 ml vegetable stock
150 g silken tofu
1 tbsp tomato puree
6 sun-dried tomatoes from a jar
1 tsp paprika
340g dried vegan pasta
Fresh parsley, finely chopped, to serve
Chilli flakes, to serve

Preheat the oven to 220C/Fan 200C/Gas Mark 7 (400F/390F). Place the sliced carrots and onion in a large baking tray and drizzle with half of the olive oil. Add the miso paste and massage into the carrots with your hands. Sprinkle with the salt and black pepper. Slice the bulb of garlic in half horizontally and place it in the centre of the carrots, cut-sides up. Drizzle with the remaining olive oil. Place the tray in the oven to roast for 30 min, tossing the carrots and onion half-way through cooking, or until the carrots are tender and slightly browned. Remove from the oven and allow to cool for 5 min. Squeeze the soft roasted garlic cloves out of their skins into the tray with the carrots. In a blender, combine the roasted carrots, onions and garlic with the vegetable stock, silken tofu, tomato puree, sun-dried tomatoes and paprika, and season with salt and black pepper. Blend until smooth and creamy. Cook the pasta in a large saucepan of salted water until it's al dente. Reserving around a cup of the pasta water, drain and return the pasta to the pan. Pour the creamy sauce over the pasta and place the saucepan back over medium heat. If necessary, use the reserved pasta water to loosen the sauce. Stir until just piping hot, then divide the pasta amongst four bowls. Serve immediately, sprinkled with fresh parsley and chilli flakes.

Notes: Dunno where he found 4 carrots = 450g, but it's roughly 1 lb. Original recipe says serves 2, however used a 450g package of dried pasta and was enough for 4-6 easily. Used chicken stock, used peeled garlic cloves, works fine. Had to add another 1/2 c of liquid to get it to blend, used probably 1/2-3/4 c pasta water and was still thick. also, typical block of tofu is ~453 g. 

